Topic: Which neighborhood rain gardens to select for a joint flood resilience field study
Disciplines: hydrology, civil engineering, environmental economics
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Professor Luis Ortega: Thanks for squeezing into the lab room. With equipment due May 22, I’d like us to leave with two sites the crew can actually instrument.

Dr. Maya Chen: Right. Willow and Maple are both strong on the storms we measured; I’d like to check the heavier events for gaps before we lock them in.

Dr. Amina Okafor: That’d help. For those heavier storms, could we check whether the nearby blocks had street closures? I’d like to know whether the strong garden measurements line up with disruptions people actually experienced.

Dr. Maya Chen: Yeah, I can match the heavier storms in the gauge file to closure dates. Amina, could you check whether those closures were on the blocks draining toward Willow and Maple, rather than just nearby?

Dr. Amina Okafor: Yeah, I can map those closure locations against Luis’s drainage connections. The closure records may not show which direction the water came from, though.

Professor Luis Ortega: Right, my survey traces the pipe connections, not where water moves across the street when it ponds. If you send me the closure locations, I can check them against the inlet elevations and low points.

Dr. Maya Chen: That would help. While you’re looking at the low points, could you flag any route where water might have gone around our inlet sensors during the heavier storms? That could change how I read those reductions.

Professor Luis Ortega: Yeah, I’ll check the parallel catch basins upstream of each sensor. If they start taking flow in heavier storms, our measured inlet volume may not represent the same drainage area.

Dr. Amina Okafor: If those parallel basins take flow, could you mark which blocks they serve? That’d help me pick comparison blocks with similar flood exposure, not just nearby streets.

Professor Luis Ortega: Yeah, I can mark the tributary blocks for those basins. Some curb inlets sit right at drainage-area boundaries, though, so I’ll flag those rather than draw a hard line.

Dr. Amina Okafor: Okay, the flagged boundary blocks can stay out of the comparison for now. I can use the 311 calls to find places to ask residents and businesses about missed work or closures, rather than treating call counts as the disruption measure.

Professor Luis Ortega: Yeah, those conversations might also tell us where to check for recurring inlet clogging. A monitor can keep logging while a grate fills with debris, and then we’re measuring a maintenance problem as much as the garden.

Dr. Maya Chen: Mm, do your site notes say whether either grate was clogged during our monitored storms? I can flag those events in the storm comparison.

Professor Luis Ortega: Not event by event, no—I inspected after those storms. I’ll check the crew photos for debris at either grate, but I wouldn’t flag a storm as clogged from my notes alone.

Dr. Maya Chen: Okay, I won’t mark any storm as clogged without dated evidence. I’ll also check the gauge file for larger storms we missed entirely at Willow or Maple, so we know what conditions those reductions actually cover.

Dr. Amina Okafor: Could you send me the dates of those missed storms? I’ll check whether other drainage work was underway near Willow or Maple then, so we know what residents might be remembering.

Dr. Maya Chen: Sure, I’ll send the missed-storm dates, with the gauge totals so you can see how big they were. That might help separate drainage work from storm conditions.

Dr. Amina Okafor: Great, the totals will help me pick a couple of comparable storms to ask residents and businesses about, rather than just asking whether they’ve had flooding.

Professor Luis Ortega: When you ask about those storms, could you distinguish street ponding from basement sewer backups? Those may be different hydraulic pathways, and my connection survey wouldn’t tell us which residents experienced.

Dr. Amina Okafor: Yeah, I’ll ask about those separately. For street ponding, I’ll also ask whether it blocked someone’s usual route to work—that gets us closer to the disruption we’re trying to measure.

Dr. Maya Chen: For those route questions, I can send the storm times, not just dates. Whether the street was blocked during the morning commute or later might matter.

Dr. Amina Okafor: Yeah, please send the times. I can ask about trips people actually made during those hours, including evening shifts, rather than assuming everyone commutes in the morning.

Dr. Maya Chen: Right, I’ll send the start and end times too, so you can match trips to the storm.

Professor Luis Ortega: I think we should confirm Willow and Maple for the May 22 reservation; the crew can install and service monitors at both. If the closure mapping shows we’re looking at different drainage paths, though, let’s catch that before installation.

Dr. Amina Okafor: Okay, let’s reserve Willow and Maple. I’ll map the closures against your low points before installation and flag it quickly if either garden doesn’t connect to the blocks where people were disrupted.

Dr. Maya Chen: Okay, I’ll send the storm times and missed-event dates tomorrow. If the heavier-storm checks change how I read either site, I’ll flag it before installation.

